==Latin==

===Etymology===
From {{der|la|ine-pro|*h₂ewti||on the other hand}}, from {{m|ine-pro|*h₂ew-}}. Cognate with Ancient Greek {{l|grc|αὖ}}, {{l|grc|αὖτε}}, Gothic {{m|got|𐌰𐌿𐌸𐍃|tr=auths|sc=Goth}}. Confer with {{cog|la|autem}}, {{cog|grc|αὐτός}}, {{m|grc|αὐτάρ}}.

===Conjunction===
{{head|la|conjunction}}

# [[or]] ([[exclusive or]])
#: {{ux|la|Marcus ludos videbit '''aut''' dormiet.|Marcus will watch the games '''or''' sleep [but not both].}}
#: {{ux|la|'''Aut''' Caesar, '''aut''' nihil.|'''Either''' Caesar '''or''' nothing (''figuratively:'' all or nothing)}}
#: {{ux|la|'''Aut''' disce '''aut''' discede.|'''Either''' learn '''or''' go away.}}
#*: '''4th century''', [[w:St Jerome|St Jerome]], [[w:Vulgate|Vulgate]], [[Tobit#Etymology 1|Tobit]] 3:19:
#*:: {{l|la||et '''aut''' ego indigna fui illis '''aut''' illi mihi forsitan digni non fuerunt quia forsitan viro alio conservasti me}}
#*::: And '''either''' I was unworthy of them, '''or''' they perhaps were not worthy of me: because perhaps thou hast kept me for another man,

====Usage notes====
* This word is used in pairs (''[[aut]]'' ... ''[[aut]]'') to mean "{{m|en|either}}....{{m|en|or}}".
* Unlike {{m|la|vel}}, this word implies an exclusive "or"; i.e., one option or the other, but not both. (A similiar distinction is made in Polish, compare {{m|pl|albo}} and {{m|pl|lub}}.)

